---
import HardwareNote from "../../layouts/HardwareNote.astro";
import { url, evidenceUrl, evidenceAsset } from "../../lib/specs";
import HwSection from "../../components/HwSection.astro";
import HwFigure from "../../components/HwFigure.astro";
import HwNote from "../../components/HwNote.astro";

// Every photograph on this page is a file committed under
// docs/evidence/panel-photos/. `scripts/render_specs.py` finds these paths in
// this source, copies the image into the site and gives it a page of its own,
// so the caption and the file a reader can open are the same thing.
const shots = [
  {
    path: "docs/evidence/panel-photos/01-urandom-noise.jpg",
    wrote: "dd if=/dev/urandom",
    saw: "bright field, visible speckle",
  },
  {
    path: "docs/evidence/panel-photos/02-all-black.jpg",
    wrote: "dd if=/dev/zero",
    saw: "dark",
  },
  {
    path: "docs/evidence/panel-photos/03-all-white.jpg",
    wrote: "0xFF bytes",
    saw: "bright band",
  },
];

// The sequence the board actually sends, quoted from
// nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi. Framing is {data type, delay in
// milliseconds, payload length}, then the payload. <b> marks what the dead
// sequence we had been sending did not contain at all.
const initSeq = `panel-init-sequence = [
    15 03 02 fe fd            Raydium page select: FE=FD
    15 03 02 80 fc            page FC
    15 03 02 fe 00            back to the user page 00
<b>    39 03 05 2a 00 00 02 37   column window, 0..567</b>
<b>    39 03 05 2b 00 00 04 cf   row window, 0..1231</b>
    15 03 02 13 00            ENTER_NORMAL_MODE
    15 03 02 35 00            tearing effect on
<b>    15 05 02 51 fe            SET_DISPLAY_BRIGHTNESS 0xFE, then 5 ms</b>
<b>    05 7b 01 11               SLEEP_OUT, then 123 ms</b>
    05 03 01 29               DISPLAY_ON
    15 03 02 3a 77            pixel format
];`;
---

<HardwareNote
  slug="panel-lit"
  title="The panel lights, and what was keeping it dark"
  eyebrow="LILYGO T-Display-K230 · hardware note · observed"
  dek="On 2026&#8209;09&#8209;21 the RM69A10 emitted light under Linux, and the kernel console rendered on the glass. The panel had never been deaf: it had been fully initialised by <em>a different panel's</em> init sequence, and never told to leave zero brightness."
  description="On 2026-09-21 the RM69A10 AMOLED on the LILYGO T-Display-K230 emitted light under Linux and the kernel console rendered on it. Photographs, the transcription error that kept it dark, and the regression that blanked it again."
  source="docs/evidence/panel-lit.md"
  meta={[
    { label: "Observed", value: "2026-09-21<br>webcam, photographs committed" },
    { label: "Panel", value: "RM69A10 568&times;1232 AMOLED<br>MIPI DSI, 2 lanes, 24 bpp" },
    { label: "Console", value: "71&times;77 characters<br>fbcon on <code>/dev/fb0</code>, 8&times;16 font" },
    { label: "Init sequence", value: "11 commands, 26 payload bytes<br>the vendor's live function, not its dead one" },
    { label: "Not settled", value: "~3 rows of vertical motion<br>and no requirement rests on this yet" },
  ]}
>
  <HwSection kind="the photographs" title="Three states, because one proves nothing">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      A single bright photograph of a screen could be a reflection, and a single
      dark one is exactly what a dead panel looks like. So the framebuffer was
      driven to three known states from a shell on the board and each was
      photographed. <strong>The change is the evidence</strong>, not any one
      frame.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-shots">
      {
        shots.map((s) => (
          <figure>
            <a href={evidenceUrl(s.path)}>
              <img src={evidenceAsset(s.path)} alt={`The panel while ${s.wrote} was written to /dev/fb0: ${s.saw}.`} loading="lazy" />
            </a>
            <figcaption>
              <code>{s.wrote}</code>
              <span>{s.saw}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="hw-p">
      Writing a pattern at all was necessary, and it is worth saying why: an
      all&#8209;zero framebuffer on a working panel is indistinguishable from a
      panel that is off, and for most of this investigation the framebuffer was
      zeroed. Several days of &ldquo;the screen is dark&rdquo; contained an
      unknown amount of &ldquo;nothing was ever drawn&rdquo;.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the cause" title="We transcribed the function nothing calls">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      The board is driven by the kernel's generic Canaan panel support, which
      takes its initialisation sequence from the device tree. That sequence was
      transcribed from LilyGO's RT&#8209;Smart driver for this exact panel on
      this exact board &mdash; and from the wrong function in it.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-contract">
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-head">
        <span>in <code>rm69a10.c</code></span>
        <span>what it is</span>
        <span>what that meant</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <div class="a">rm69a10_OLD_init()<br />line 39</div>
        <div class="b">no callers anywhere in the tree; byte&#8209;for&#8209;byte identical to <code>hx8399_v2_init()</code></div>
        <div class="c">
          <strong>This is what we had been sending</strong> &mdash; all 20
          commands and 297 payload bytes of it, transcribed faithfully and
          verified byte&#8209;for&#8209;byte against the vendor source. It is
          the init sequence for a Himax HX8399, a 1080&times;1920 panel that is
          not on this board. It was reachable, plausible, and dead.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <div class="a">rm69a10_568x1232_init()<br />line 96</div>
        <div class="b">what the dispatcher at lines 345 and 347 actually calls</div>
        <div class="c">
          <strong>This is the live one.</strong> 13 commands, 36 bytes, and it
          contains three things the Himax sequence does not.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="hw-p">
      What the board sends now is below, quoted from
      <code>nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi</code>. It follows the live
      vendor function with one deliberate difference, which is marked further
      down: the vendor's partial-area block is replaced by
      <code>ENTER_NORMAL_MODE</code>. Every packet also carries the 3 ms the
      vendor's <code>dwc_lpdt_send_pkg()</code> waits after each one and ours
      did not.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={initSeq} />

    <ul class="hw-key">
      <li>
        <i class="k-ours"></i>
        <span
          ><code>51 FE</code> &mdash; <code>SET_DISPLAY_BRIGHTNESS</code>. A
          DCS&#8209;brightness AMOLED powers up at zero. It can be completely
          and correctly initialised, accept every command, report itself
          healthy, and emit nothing. This is almost certainly the single line
          that was keeping the screen dark.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <i class="k-ours"></i>
        <span
          ><code>2A</code> / <code>2B</code> &mdash; the frame memory window,
          which the Himax sequence never sets for this geometry.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <i class="k-ours"></i>
        <span
          >120&nbsp;ms after <code>SLEEP_OUT</code>, where the wrong sequence
          allowed 1&nbsp;ms.</span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="hw-p">
      The full transcription, command by command, with what each one is for, is
      in <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rm69a10-init-sequence.md")}
        ><code>docs/evidence/rm69a10-init-sequence.md</code></a
      >; the comparison against how the vendor firmware drives the same part is
      in <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rtsmart-panel-bringup.md")}
        ><code>docs/evidence/rtsmart-panel-bringup.md</code></a
      >.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the console" title="71&times;77 characters on the glass">
    <HwFigure
      bare
      caption="<strong>docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg</strong> &mdash; the kernel console rendering on the panel, photographed off the board."
    >
      <div class="hw-shot-wide">
        <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg")}>
          <img
            src={evidenceAsset("docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg")}
            alt="The board's panel showing the Linux kernel console as white text on black."
          />
        </a>
      </div>
    </HwFigure>

    <pre class="hw-code">[0.174613] fbcon: Taking over console
[3.665931] <b>Console: switching to colour frame buffer device 71x77</b></pre>

    <p class="hw-p">
      71&times;77 characters is 568/8 by 1232/16: the whole panel at an
      8&times;16 font, which is the arithmetic confirming that fbcon has the
      real geometry and not a fallback. Two changes were needed, and one of
      them is a trap worth naming.
    </p>

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="console=tty0 goes first, not last">
      <p>
        The <em>last</em> <code>console=</code> on the kernel command line
        becomes <code>/dev/console</code>. <code>ttyS0</code> has to stay
        primary, so <code>console=tty0</code> is added with
        <code>lib.mkBefore</code>. Losing the serial console on a board in the
        middle of bring&#8209;up would be a bad trade: every diagnosis on this
        page was made over it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE=y</code> also had to go back. It had
        been switched off while testing whether fbcon's console take&#8209;over
        was holding <code>console_lock</code> across a boot hang. It was not
        &mdash; the board hung identically without it, and the cause was an
        unbounded wait in the DSI PHY.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the regression" title="A driver's opinion of itself is not evidence">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Hours after the photographs above, the panel went dark again and stayed
      dark across reboots and a full power cycle. Every status bit the driver
      exposes said the display was on. DRM reported the connector
      <code>connected</code>, the CRTC <code>enabled</code>, and
      <code>dpms On</code>. All of it was true, and none of it was worth
      anything.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>hypothesis</th><th>test, on the board</th><th>result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">the framebuffer is all zeroes</th>
            <td>wrote 1.4 MB of <code>/dev/urandom</code>, then all&#8209;<code>0xFF</code>, to <code>/dev/fb0</code></td>
            <td class="remark">still black</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">the pushed touch DTB broke it</th>
            <td>decompiled both DTBs and diffed them</td>
            <td class="remark">only the interrupt type and <code>touchscreen-size-*</code> differ; zero display deltas</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">&hellip;still the DTB</th>
            <td>pushed the image's original DTB back and rebooted</td>
            <td class="remark">still black</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">console blanking</th>
            <td>black 35 s after a cold boot; the default is 600 s</td>
            <td class="remark">not blanking</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">panel power or reset GPIO</th>
            <td><code>/sys/kernel/debug/gpio</code></td>
            <td class="remark"><code>dsi_reset</code> hi, <code>backlight_gpio</code> hi</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">display power domain off</th>
            <td><code>pm_genpd_summary</code></td>
            <td class="remark"><code>disp_domain on</code>, subsystem active</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">display clocks gated</th>
            <td><code>clk_summary</code></td>
            <td class="remark">all <code>disp_*</code> enabled</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">the panel latched in a bad state</th>
            <td>power-cycled the board by hand</td>
            <td class="remark">still black</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="hw-p">
      What broke the tie was the persistent journal.
      <code>journalctl --list-boots</code> held all four boots of that card, and
      the display lines are byte&#8209;identical in every one of them &mdash;
      including the first. So <em>that card had never lit the panel</em>, which
      dates the regression to the image rather than to anything done at runtime,
      and leaves exactly one kernel&#8209;affecting commit between the last lit
      image and this one: a patch giving the DSI link 25% burst headroom, built
      at 17:12 and never visually confirmed.
    </p>

    <HwNote kind="grounded" label="The confirming experiment, and what it cost">
      <p>
        A kernel patch cannot be pushed over serial, but the PHY clock is
        derived from the device tree's pixel clock &mdash;
        <code>phy_clk_freq = pclk &times; 3 &times; 8 / lanes / 2</code>, which
        for 2 lanes at 24 bpp is <code>pclk &times; 6</code>, and then
        <code>&times; 5/4</code> for the patch. Scaling
        <code>clock-frequency</code> down by 4/5 cancels it exactly.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the shipped 49.5 MHz (371.25 MHz on the link) the panel was black. At
        31.68 MHz &mdash; 237.6 MHz, the rate that had been lit &mdash; it lit
        and the console was legible, and <em>every scanline was sheared
        progressively to the right</em>. That is a line&#8209;length mismatch:
        the VO's pixel timing and the DSI byte clock are not independent in this
        driver, nothing recomputes the video-mode registers when the PHY moves,
        and so no device-tree value can work around the patch. It was reverted.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwFigure
      bare
      caption="<strong>docs/evidence/panel-photos/06-sheared-console-31.68mhz.jpg</strong> &mdash; the same console at the cancelling pixel clock. Lit, legible, and sheared: this photograph is what diagnosed the clock, and is not a picture of the panel working."
    >
      <div class="hw-shot-wide">
        <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-photos/06-sheared-console-31.68mhz.jpg")}>
          <img
            src={evidenceAsset("docs/evidence/panel-photos/06-sheared-console-31.68mhz.jpg")}
            alt="The panel showing console text sheared progressively to the right, drawing diagonal bands across the frame."
          />
        </a>
      </div>
    </HwFigure>

    <p class="hw-p">
      There is a postscript in
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/dsi-burst-headroom.md")}
        ><code>docs/evidence/dsi-burst-headroom.md</code></a
      >
      worth reading if you ever multiply a clock: the patch also moved the PHY's
      VCO range. <code>phy_clk_freq</code> is in kHz and immediately feeds a
      ladder of comparisons that pick the VCO bucket, so 297 000 landed under
      <code>&lt; 330000</code> and 371 250 under <code>&lt; 440000</code>. The
      original commit called that out as intended, and it was intended, and the
      patch still broke the picture &mdash; because nothing moved the VO's pixel
      timing to match.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="what is not known" title="Lit is not working">
    <div class="hw-q">
      <div>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The brightness flicker is largely gone. Something still moves.</h3>
          <p>
            Remeasured on the corrected image &mdash; headroom patch removed,
            49.5 MHz pixel clock, LILYGO 52.19 Hz timings, lit and unsheared
            &mdash; over 30 s of webcam video at a locked manual exposure,
            because otherwise what gets measured is the camera's gain control.
            Brightness dips below 90% of the mean: <strong>none</strong>, against
            a baseline of 19 dips in 30 s with the worst at 53%. Two dips fall
            below 95%, which is what &ldquo;it flickers sometimes&rdquo; looks
            like as a number.
          </p>
          <p>
            Vertical motion remains, about 3 panel rows out of 1232, seen
            directly as bands moving in unison with no tearing.
            <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/flicker-after-headroom-revert.md")}
              >The measurement note</a
            > is worth reading for what it refuses to conclude: the apparent
            displacement grows 9.3&times; down the frame, which looks exactly
            like timing error accumulating from a vsync anchor &mdash; and the
            panel is shot at an oblique angle whose perspective magnification is
            5.6&times; across the same strip, in the same direction. Uniform
            shift and accumulation are not distinguished by that footage, so
            neither is claimed. Warping the frames into true panel space would
            remove the perspective term and is the current attempt; the first
            try produced a neatly rectified photograph of the worktop, and the
            second had the near and far ends of the panel the wrong way round.
            Both are written up, which is the useful part.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>We do not drive the VO the way the vendor does.</h3>
          <p>
            One divergence is deliberate and settled: the vendor ends its
            sequence <code>31</code> / <code>30</code> / <code>12 00</code>
            &mdash; partial columns, partial rows,
            <code>ENTER_PARTIAL_MODE</code> over a restricted area &mdash; and
            copying that exactly lit the panel but banded the image, which is
            what a restricted partial area looks like when the VO streams a
            full frame into it. <code>ENTER_NORMAL_MODE</code> replaced it, and
            <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-photos/04-normal-mode-fullscreen-noise.jpg")}>photograph 04</a>
            is the full-screen result. The divergences that
            remain are listed in priority order in the bring&#8209;up note: no
            SoC display reset on enable, a stale reset pulse,
            <code>vco_cntrl</code> 0x19 against the 0x17 Linux computes, and
            CRTC&#8209;before&#8209;encoder ordering that streams video into a
            link that is not configured yet.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A failed DCS read is not proof the panel is deaf.</h3>
          <p>
            <code>RDDPM (0x0A)</code> still times out &mdash; <code>-110</code>,
            <code>CMD_PKT_STATUS 0x50015</code>, <code>GEN_PLD_R_EMPTY</code>
            set &mdash; on a panel that is visibly working. Earlier in this
            investigation that timeout was read as strong evidence the panel was
            receiving nothing. That inference is now known to be unsafe, and the
            correction belongs on the record next to the conclusion it spoiled.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">4</span>
        <div>
          <h3>What the corrected image does, and what it is still not.</h3>
          <p>
            Reverting restores <code>pclk &times; 6</code> at the LILYGO
            52.19 Hz timings &mdash; 297 MHz on the link, the rate the console
            photograph above was taken at &mdash; and that image has since been
            built, flashed and measured: lit, no shear, and the brightness
            numbers in the item above. So the panel currently in the board is
            working in the sense that light comes out of it and the console is
            legible.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is not working in the sense a requirement would need. Nothing has
            been drawn at a known position and photographed against it, the
            residual motion is unexplained, and the panel's colour, orientation
            and refresh have never been checked against anything. Lit is a long
            way from correct, and this note claims only the first.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="No display requirement is in the ledger">
      <p>
        The count on the <a href={url("")}>front page</a> does not include the
        panel. Requirements reach that count when a change is archived into
        <code>openspec/specs/</code>, and the display work is still in flight
        &mdash; this site does not publish proposals, because a published draft
        gets read as a decision. Everything above is an observation with a file
        behind it, which is not the same thing as a requirement this project has
        committed to.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Evidence: <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-lit.md")}><code>docs/evidence/panel-lit.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/dsi-burst-headroom.md")}><code>docs/evidence/dsi-burst-headroom.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-dark.md")}><code>docs/evidence/panel-dark.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/dsi-phy-hang.md")}><code>docs/evidence/dsi-phy-hang.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rm69a10-init-sequence.md")}><code>docs/evidence/rm69a10-init-sequence.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rtsmart-panel-bringup.md")}><code>docs/evidence/rtsmart-panel-bringup.md</code></a>,
      and the photographs under <code>docs/evidence/panel-photos/</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vendor source read for this note: <code>rm69a10.c</code> and
      <code>gt9895.c</code> in LilyGO's RT&#8209;Smart tree, and
      <code>canaan_dsi.c</code> and
      <code>drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-canaan-universal.c</code> in the pinned
      Xuantie kernel. Neither tree is committed here &mdash; they are large
      vendor clones &mdash; so those paths are named but not linked.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
